;; A yield point in a mutually recursive pair of body-local functions.
;;
;; This is the shape that made the old message wrong. A body-local function may
;; suspend now — `158_local_functions.bjo` — but a *recursive* one is not a
;; binding by the time colour is decided: `LetRecify` and `LoopLowering` turn it
;; into a loop, and a loop either becomes a `while` in the enclosing member or
;; stays a group of C# local functions. Only the first can suspend.
;;
;; What decides is tail position, not recursion. `evens` calls `odds` inside a
;; `+`, so it is a call rather than a jump, and a call needs a real function to
;; refer to.
;;
;; The message had been "'evens' is a loop, but it still mentions its own name
;; ... Stop using 'evens' as a value and it becomes one". Both halves are false
;; here: `evens` mentions `odds`, not itself, and neither is used as a value
;; anywhere — so the reader was sent looking for something that is not in the
;; program. It now names `odds`, which is the one that cannot be jumped to.
;;
;; Both fixes it offers are checked. Making the mention a tail call is
;; `158_local_functions.bjo`'s `parity`, which compiles; lifting to a top-level
;; `defbjo` is what `errors/suspend_in_recursive_local_function.bjo` describes.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: calling 'fetch' is a yield point
;; EXPECT-ERROR: 'evens' is one of a group of loops
;; EXPECT-ERROR: 'odds' is mentioned somewhere that is not a jump
;; EXPECT-ERROR: make every mention of 'odds' a tail call

(: fetch (-> int int))
(defbjo (fetch x) (* x 2))

(: outer (-> int int))
(defbjo (outer n)
  (defun (evens i) (if (<= i 0) 0 (+ (fetch i) (odds (- i 1)))))
  (defun (odds i) (if (<= i 0) 0 (evens (- i 1))))
  (evens n))

(defun (main args) 0)
